  "evaluation": "C and B correctly classify the disagreement as genuine, identify population as the clearest reason, and note the differences in depression measures and study design. C is marginally stronger because its detail avoids ambiguity between Study A's enrolled sample and its 99 analyzed participants; B's reported n = 126 is nevertheless supplied in the fields. A incorrectly calls the disagreement apparent by inferring inadequate power from sample size and the small effect in B. The rules require an explicit pilot/underpowered signal or a confidence interval encompassing the other effect, neither of which is provided for A.",
  "ranking": ["C", "B", "A"]
}
